\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The cortico-thalamo-cortical loop and the canonical cortical column, traced as a numbered flow. (1)~Sensory input reaches a first-order relay, which (2)~drives layer~4. (3--4)~The column processes it through the canonical intracolumnar flow (L4~$\rightarrow $~L2/3~$\rightarrow $~L5). (5--6)~Layer~5 sends a driving projection to a higher-order relay, which drives layer~4 of the next cortical area (the transthalamic route). Layer~6 is drawn as its two pyramidal populations. Layer~5 projects to both corticothalamic (CT) and corticocortical (CC) cells, and CC cells in turn excite CT cells (thin, intracortical). CT cells also receive a collateral of the first-order relay's thalamocortical axon, so that they combine the column's processed output with the relay's own input. Only the CT cells return a modulatory projection (thin grey) to both relays and the TRN, and the TRN gates both relays (blue, bar-ended). Direct corticocortical projections (dashed, class open) run alongside the transthalamic route: feedforward from L2/3 to layer~4 of the next area, and top-down feedback from a higher cortical area onto the apical tufts of L2/3 and L5 pyramidal cells in layer~1. The relays' collateral input to the TRN and the long horizontal axons of CC cells are omitted for clarity. Connection classes follow Sherman and Guillery~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {sherman1998, sherman2013}; the organization of layer~6 follows Thomson~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {thomson2010layer6}.}}{14}{figure.1.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:circuit}{{1.2}{14}{The cortico-thalamo-cortical loop and the canonical cortical column, traced as a numbered flow. (1)~Sensory input reaches a first-order relay, which (2)~drives layer~4. (3--4)~The column processes it through the canonical intracolumnar flow (L4~$\rightarrow $~L2/3~$\rightarrow $~L5). (5--6)~Layer~5 sends a driving projection to a higher-order relay, which drives layer~4 of the next cortical area (the transthalamic route). Layer~6 is drawn as its two pyramidal populations. Layer~5 projects to both corticothalamic (CT) and corticocortical (CC) cells, and CC cells in turn excite CT cells (thin, intracortical). CT cells also receive a collateral of the first-order relay's thalamocortical axon, so that they combine the column's processed output with the relay's own input. Only the CT cells return a modulatory projection (thin grey) to both relays and the TRN, and the TRN gates both relays (blue, bar-ended). Direct corticocortical projections (dashed, class open) run alongside the transthalamic route: feedforward from L2/3 to layer~4 of the next area, and top-down feedback from a higher cortical area onto the apical tufts of L2/3 and L5 pyramidal cells in layer~1. The relays' collateral input to the TRN and the long horizontal axons of CC cells are omitted for clarity. Connection classes follow Sherman and Guillery~\cite {sherman1998, sherman2013}; the organization of layer~6 follows Thomson~\cite {thomson2010layer6}}{figure.1.2}{}}
